      "math_academy_id": "q-42576",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(4\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 5)\\cdot (1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-40)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
